ceos have a reputation of being  douchebags they mistreat their employees  exploit every tax loophole known to  mankind and they aren't scared to put  profits before ethics basically every  single well-known founder has this  reputation including jeff bezos bill  gates and mark zuckerberg even the most  cherished of the bunch like elon musk  display many of these characteristics  i mean 80 to 100 hour weeks are  basically the definition of tesla and  spacex's work cultures  many have argued that though these ceos  have produced tremendous results it  wasn't without sacrificing their  humanity and it often seems like such  results are simply not possible without  being a brutal businessman  but in reality hundreds of ceos defied  this notion with one of the most notable  being southwest ceo gary c kelly  in 2021 gary ranked number four on  gloucester's employee choice list with  an approval rating of 98  gary hasn't dropped the ball on  investors either since he took over  southwest stock has quadrupled at the  peak and let's keep in mind this isn't a  tech company it's an airline company  so here's how gary stayed on the good  side of his employees while growing  southwest and one of the most cutthroat  and low profit industries in the world  taking a look back gary joined southwest  way back in 1986 as a modest controller  not to be confused with a flight  controller by trade gary was an  accountant who graduated from the  university of texas at austin  before joining southwest gary had  actually been working at an auditing  company called arthur young and co  so becoming an accounting controller at  southwest was right up his alley at the  time southwest was a rookie in the  airline business who was simply trying  to get ahead the company had just gone  public a few years ago and each share of  the company was worth less than a dollar  in comparison to today's prices given  the small scale nature of southwest it  wasn't too difficult for gary to work  himself up to cfo by 1989  and this is where he would spend the  majority of his early career it wasn't  until 2001 that gary was promoted to  executive vice president but his next  promotion came rather quickly in 2004 to  ceo  since he became ceo he's embraced a  couple of key fundamental values that  have cemented southwest in the hearts of  its employees and customers starting  with a culture of appreciation in large  companies that employ tens of thousands  of employees the individual impact of  each employee is often lost every  employee is simply a cog in the larger  machine and it often feels like the  dispensable slash replaceable but gary  has made sure to emphasize that this is  not the case  and i don't mean just verbally thinking  or praising employees gary has backed up  his words with clear action throughout  southwest entire history they have never  laid off a single employee not after 9  11 not during the financial crisis and  not even during the pandemic this  doesn't mean that southwest just flew  through these crises though they were  actually extremely challenging and  southwest had to enact several  cost-cutting measures however layoffs  and pay cuts to regular employees were  always a last resort i mean just take a  look at how gary handled the pandemic  when the pandemic rolled around it was  clear that southwest had to make massive  reductions in cost given that air travel  demand literally went to zero overnight  instead of laying off employees though  kerry targeted the other side of the  spectrum employees that were over 55 and  approaching retirement  gary offered pilots over the age of 55  two-thirds of their salaries for five  years or until they were 65 for simply  choosing early retirement on top of this  extremely generous compensation plan  gary also offered up to five years of  company provided health insurance  meanwhile employees who were under 55  but had over 10 years of experience with  southwest were offered one year of  salary and one year of company paid  health insurance given how lucrative  this offer was 17 000 employees or 28 of  their workforce took their buyout which  was quite close to gary's 30 target i  think you'd agree that this was  literally the most generous way  southwest could have reduced the  workforce on top of giving employees a  great send-off gary and all the  executives took a pay cut through the  end of 2021  gary's salary was cut to zero dollars  while the other executive salaries were  slashed by 20 percent gary also needed  regular employees to take a pay cut as  well but he didn't want to impose it  so he promised that employees who took a  10 pay cut would have zero risk of being  full load in 2021 and that their pay  would be restored normal starting in  2022 throughout the entire crisis there  was no force or imposing the approach  was always how do we get through this as  a team  maybe older employees can take early  retirement maybe executives can take a  larger pay cut and maybe employees can  take a smaller pay cut southwest has  approached every crisis with this same  team-like approach which has made the  community extremely strong and that  brings us to gary's second strategy  which is breaking down the executive  barrier  as companies grow larger they often have  a tendency to become bureaucratic and  the key reason for this is little to no  decision making power for everyday  employees at southwest however the  decision-making process is completely  decentralized as we saw with the  pandemic it was up to the employees if  they wanted to take a 10 pay cut and  avoid a furlough or if they wanted to  keep their full salary and deal with the  risk of a potential furlough this  decentralization stretches much further  than just allowing employees to choose  their own futures a great example of  this was when southwest was developing a  new uniform for their employees  pilot and flight attendant uniforms are  extremely important as this is what  customers see on a daily basis and it's  essentially the branding of the entire  company given the weight of this  redesign you would think that southwest  would hire top-tier branding specialists  to guide the process but gary had  something else in mind  gary opened up to redesign opportunity  to all southwest employees every  employee who was interested could  provide suggestions for the new uniform  and the employees with the best  suggestions were assembled into a sort  of review team every two weeks the team  would meet up and guide the redesign  process  similarly a significant amount of  decision-making power is given to  low-level managers and employees  gary believes that these individuals are  the ones that have the best  understanding of the work they do and  that they can make way better decisions  than some executive that spends their  entire day in the office  all of this is based on gary's core idea  that employee satisfaction trumps  everything even including customer  satisfaction the truth is customer  satisfaction is directly linked to  employee satisfaction so if you keep  your employees happy customer  satisfaction will follow this doesn't  mean that gary doesn't care about  customers though if anything he cares  about each individual customer just as  much as he cares about each individual  employee if you've ever taken a  southwest flight you might have noticed  that they don't have business or first  class they only have economy originally  this was because southwest was a budget  airline and premium flyers weren't  interested in the company but today  southwest actively chooses against  adding first and business class  southwest does offer something called a  business select but this doesn't  actually get you a better seat or  service  business select simply allows you to  move up the boarding list and it gives  you some additional member rewards  however you'll be sitting in the same  seat and receiving the same service as  someone with an economy ticket by opting  for no first or business class gary has  been able to prevent an executive  barrier from farming amongst customers  and service  it's no secret that most airlines make  the majority of their profit from  business and first class fliers thanks  to how much margin is built into those  services  however this creates large discrepancy  in how customers are treated which goes  against southwest core principle of  valuing each customer equally customers  who fly southwest generally love this  aspect flight attendants aren't  prioritizing business and first class  members and economy customers are the  center of attention now i'm not saying  it's wrong to prioritize business and  first-class customers given that they're  paying way more  however by simply sticking to economy  customers southwest is able to provide  an extremely consistent and reliable  experience to all customers  and speaking of happy customers we have  gary's third strategy which is not  nickeling and diming customers or  employees  ever since 9 11 and especially after the  2008 financial crisis more and more  american airlines have been charging  additional fees on top of the ticket  price for checked bags  and as more airlines adopt a strategy it  becomes harder to compete against them  because they're able to advertise  cheaper starting prices but the truth is  these cheaper prices are deceptively low  considering that most people are going  to need to check in some luggage  southwest has avoided this by simply  allowing all customers to check in two  bags for no additional cost this has  actually become southwest most famous  marketing slogan and is an integral part  of their brand today  unfortunately southwest can't actually  offer the service for free as it costs  money to transport luggage this leads us  the southwest starting prices to  generally be a bit higher than  competitors but southwest customers can  be confident that the ticket price is  all they'll have to pay there aren't any  hidden or additional fees that they'll  find out about when they arrive at the  airport this policy also extends to  other parts of southwest business for  example they don't charge any change  fees or cancellation fees if you want to  switch from one ticket to another you'll  only have to pay the disparity and take  a price if there is one by not nickeling  and diming customers gary has been able  to largely eliminate customer complaints  in fact southwest only receives about  half a complaint for every 100 000  customers they serve which is by far the  lowest in the airline industry gary also  doesn't nickel and dime his employees  either right before the pandemic started  southwest dispersed 667 million worth of  profits to employees which came out to  roughly 12.2 percent of each employee's  salary 10 percent went towards the  retirement plans and the remain 2.2  percent was dispersed as cash at the end  of the day the secrets behind gary's  crazy employee and customer satisfaction  rates aren't actually all that crazy  all he does is give his employees and  customers the freedom to make their own  decisions treats everyone as equals and  he doesn't nickel and dime anyone you  would think that these principles are  pretty easy to apply  but given how many douchebag ceos there  are this clearly isn't the case  but hey at least we have a couple of  star performers like gary what do you  guys think is the biggest key to gary's  popularity amongst employees comment  that down below  also drop a like if you think all ceo  should learn a thing or two from gary  and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one
 

